# +SkillCount, +People, +Budget, -Term
# +SkillCount, +People, +Budget, -Sizes
# +SkillCount, +People, -Size
#   * <module> The smallest sufficient team: three nested layers, each to be replaced natively
#
# A family of the reduction series: a non-trivial problem with a concise
# definition whose solution needs a pattern recognised and replaced natively
# at every layer of nested subproblems. The problem is LeetCode 1125, "Smallest Sufficient Team",
# asking for the size of the team.
#
# The program writes top (corpora/library/top) where the pseudo-code and the
# argument below write {}, the empty choice of the calculus that still has
# it; doc/proofs/reduction_series_answers.md carries the argument over to
# Definition 14, and the generator is the family's Scala object in
# gezi/contest-corpora.
#
# THE PROGRAM, in continuation passing style (a set chosen once, at the head):
#
#     team ps c r k = ps (full c (k r) {})
#                        (\s. \rest. { team rest c r k,
#                                      r {} (\r'. team rest (union c s) r' k) })
#     program       = team People none T (\r. difference T r)
#
# People is a Scott list of the people's skill sets, each a bit set over the
# skills; a person is left out, or taken while the budget r of people is not
# spent.
# - the inner layer is the union of skill sets (the attractor bit_set_union);
#   replaced natively it is a bitwise or;
# - the middle layer chooses, person by person, whether to take them, and is
#   keyed by the covered set and the budget (bit_set_full at the end); many
#   choices cover the same set, so replaced natively it is the dynamic
#   programme over the subsets of skills, the least team per covered set;
# - the outer layer is the count (natural_difference, and the budget's
#   numeral case); replaced natively it is integer arithmetic.
#
# THE SNAPSHOT. S(P) is the set of the sizes at most T of the sets of people
# whose skills cover every skill: team_sizes/4. The points take T the least
# such size, so S(P) is that one number; the near miss takes T one less, so
# S(P) is empty. PROOF, with Lemma 2b of
# gezi/doc/proofs/certificate_meta_lemmas.md.
# - (⊇) For a covering set of s =< T people, choose at each person the
#   branch that takes them when they are in the set and leaves them out
#   otherwise: the budget never runs out, the covered set at the end is full,
#   and finitely many steps reach difference T (T - s), the numeral s, in
#   ⟦P⟧ by Lemma 2b.2.
# - (⊆) By Lemma 2b.3 every yield equals a pure term P reaches by a finite
#   sequence σ. The only sets of P are the pairs written above and {}; σ
#   removes a set at the head only by a member, and {} never. So a pure term
#   is reached only at the end of the list of people, through a full covered
#   set, the union of the skill sets of the people taken, their number at most
#   T (a person is taken only from a successor budget). The pure term is
#   difference T r, the number of the people taken.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - the dynamic programme over subsets: M. Held, R. M. Karp, A Dynamic
#   Programming Approach to Sequencing Problems, J. SIAM 1962,
#   doi:10.1137/0110015;
# - tabled evaluation, which keys the choice by the remaining people, the
#   covered set and the budget, so that the choices that cover the same set
#   meet: W. Chen, D. S. Warren, J. ACM 1996, doi:10.1145/227595.227597.
#
# READINGS AND SIZES: in the points' comment below (THE POINTS).
# LIMITS: the reduction family's, 10 s, 256 MB, 256 MB.

#   THE POINTS: team_point(?Point, ?SkillCount, ?PeopleCount, ?Slack), each
#   person's skills from a linear congruential sequence (seed 11, a skill
#   with probability one in four), the budget the smallest team's size plus
#   Slack. Sized by a probe of 2026-09-30 (CPUs 0-7, the whole set
#   under a 10 s limit, the budget the least size): tabling(weak_head) reads
#   6 skills and 8 people (smallest team 3) in 273 ms and 8 skills and 12
#   people (smallest 4) in 2079 ms, and is stopped at 12 skills and 24
#   people (smallest 3); weak_head reads 6 skills and 8 people in 442 ms and
#   8 skills and 12 people in 5866 ms. The near miss takes the budget one
#   short.
#
# Each text is from_cbor of a string literal, a Scott list of bytes,
# imported from corpora/library/text/cbor. Each number is from_cbor of an
# integer literal, a Scott integer, imported from corpora/library/integer/cbor.
# FIXME: the imports project "corpora" https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1923
# off the variable gezi, the name of the project root's own
# directory; it breaks if that directory is renamed.
